{"id": "nintendo_sold_zelda_ocarina_of_time", "source_key": "thegamebusiness", "source": "thegamebusiness.com", "section": "deep_analysis", "title": "Nintendo sold Zelda: Ocarina of Time to me. I’m selling it to my kids", "url": "https://www.thegamebusiness.com/p/nintendo-sold-zelda-ocarina-of-time", "published_at": "2026-11-09T17:30:54", "author": "Christopher Dring", "excerpt": "Here’s The Dring: Nostalgia is contagious, and is why I’m not concerned about Nintendo’s short-term challenges", "text": "The Game Business Show\nNintendo sold Zelda: Ocarina of Time to me. I’m selling it to my kids\n0:00\nCurrent time: 0:00 / Total time: -15:08\n-15:08\nPaid episode\nThe full episode is only available to paid subscribers of The Game Business\nNintendo sold Zelda: Ocarina of Time to me. I’m selling it to my kids\nHere’s The Dring: Nostalgia is contagious, and is why I’m not concerned about Nintendo’s short-term challenges\nChristopher Dring\n15 hours ago\n∙ Paid\nEvery Monday we do a bonus podcast/newsletter for paid supporters of The Game Business (called Here’s The Dring). This is that edition! If you like The Game Business, why not consider becoming a paid supporter? An annual sub works out at about $2/£1.53 a week. It also supports all our free episodes we do every Tuesday and Thursday. And you will also receive free access to our events, including The Game Business Live in Los Angeles.\nHi there!\nWelcome back to another week on The Game Business. The major games are starting to come out now (GTA is next week!), and so stay tuned to The Game Business on how these titles performing and, importantly, what they’re doing to console sales.\nPlus! We have interviews with Minecraft developer 4J, and two big indies tell us why they’re daring to go up against GTA 6.\nBut that’s for later in the week. For now, I’m going to analyse Nintendo’s marketing strategy for The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time remake. And give you a round-up of what’s been going on during a busy week for the industry. Enjoy!\nPlaying the long game: Generational nostalgia is Nintendo’s secret weapon\nI really like the musician Meat Loaf. I’m not making some sort of shameful admission here. I genuinely do.\nTechnically, he was a big artist when I was young. His seminal 1993 epic Bat Out of Hell II landed when I was around eight. But like a lot of fans of the Meat, that isn’t where my admiration began. It was the 1977 iconic classic Bat Out of Hell.\nI wasn’t around when that album came out, but my dad was. He was a teenager at the time, and it was one of those records that defined his youth. He played it often while I was growing up. Most vividly, I remember listening to it on the weekly drive to Scouts. I could sing along to every word of Paradise by the Dashboard Light, Two Out of Three Ain’t Bad, and the title track itself… not that I knew what exactly I was singing about (thankfully).\nJust last week, You Took The World Right Out Of My Mouth came on my playlist, and when it finished, my four-year old said he loved it. And the cycle continues.\nNostalgia is contagious. My dad listening back to his favourite album turned it into one of my favourite albums. My wife really loves the Disney Robin Hood movie, which came out 12 years before she was even born. And that’s because it was her mum’s favourite Disney film.\nI’ve been thinking about this dynamic with the launch of The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time remake. Nintendo hasn’t positioned this game at modern fans. It isn’t pitched at the Breath of the Wild crowd at all. It is aimed squarely at me. The Google ad that turns the clock back to 1998, the UK TV spot matching the exact beats of the original N64 commercial and the pre-order incentives offering replica cardboard N64 boxes… all of it targets the people who were there the first time around. It targets me.\nThe Game Business Show\nChristopher Dring hosts interviews, in-depth analysis and special features on what's truly going on in the video games industry.\nChristopher Dring hosts interviews, in-depth analysis and special features on what's truly going on in the video games industry.\nListen on\nSubstack App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nYouTube\nAppears in episode\nChristopher Dring\nRecent Episodes\nRaiders of the Lost Archive: Inside the 20,000-tape discovery that’s brought EA classics back to life\nNov 6   •   Christopher Dring\nIs AI-powered game piracy an existential threat?\nNov 5   •   Christopher Dring\nWhy Embark had to “pull the handbrake” on Arc Raiders | Full Interview\nNov 3   •   Christopher Dring\n‘Our own engine is the secret’ – How Capcom mastered the Nintendo Switch 2\nNov 2   •   Christopher Dring\nWolverine lifts PS5, but Xbox struggles despite Gears of War launch\nOct 29   •   Christopher Dring\nCan you build a legitimate video game business on prompts? Meta thinks so\nOct 27   •   Christopher Dring\n$500 million Gears of War cannot happen again\nOct 26   •   Christopher Dring", "html": "<div class=\"visibility-check\"></div><div><div><div class=\"shows-post-audio-player-wrapper-outer player-wrapper-outer-eAOhcD\"><div class=\"shows-post-audio-player-wrapper-inner player-wrapper-inner-KUdif0\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-contents pc-reset dark-theme\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-position-relative pc-reset\" style=\"min-height: 400px;\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-position-absolute pc-inset-0 pc-reset pc-borderRadius-md backgroundImage-Eijlhi\" style=\"background-image: url(&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!0GpI!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F6dde9eaf-5af8-4bf9-a870-3e1848d34ffe_3000x3000.png&quot;);\"></div><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-position-absolute pc-inset-0 pc-reset pc-borderRadius-md\" style=\"backdrop-filter: blur(120px); filter: brightness(0.7); background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(40,52,36,0.75), rgba(40,52,36,0.75)), var(--color-dark-bg-secondary);\"></div><div class=